# std.compiler.host — SH-03's production-dispatch anchor.
#
# WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS — and what changed 2026-08-08.
#
# It began as a WORKAROUND. `--lib` emitted a constructor that ran the
# runtime's initializers and `duo_register_modules()`, which initializes every
# REQUIRED module and !the module being compiled — a primary module's
# file-scope bindings were emitted as statements inside `main`, and `--lib`
# never emits `main`. So the top module of a library artifact was the one
# module that was never initialized, and compiling lib/compiler/lexer.id
# directly yielded exports that dereferenced NULL.
#
# gap[020] is FIXED: the lib constructor now runs the primary module's
# file-scope body. Measured, `lexer.id` compiled directly as the artifact
# answers a C host — n=15, first kind 14 (KIND_FUN). The anchor trick is no
# longer needed and this module is no longer load-bearing for it.
#
#     duo compile lib/compiler/host.id #backend=c #lib
#
# What it is KEPT for is the part a foreign caller cannot otherwise ask: the
# record layout it is about to decode, and a self-test that the boundary is
# live rather than returning plausible zeros. The host ABI is still the
# lexer's, not this module's.
#
# The ambient reference below is what puts the lexer on the registration path;
# an unreferenced module is not linked in.

# Bytes per token written by `duo_lexer_tokenize_all`, as i64 slots:
# kind, loc.line, loc.col, int_val. A host decoding the buffer should assert
# this rather than hard-code 4, so a layout change fails loudly at the seam.
duo_lexer_host_stride(): i64
    4
end

# One call that proves the boundary is initialized. Returns the token count
# for a fixed source, so a host can distinguish "linked and live" from "linked
# and reading uninitialized state" without knowing any Duo.
duo_lexer_host_probe(out: i64, cap: i64): i64
    std.compiler.lexer.id_lexer_tokenize_all("fun add(a: i64): i64 = a + 1 end",
        "probe.id", 1, out, cap)
end
